# Panel Balancer — Complexity

## Complexity Function

$$T(N, S, D) = I \cdot S^2 \cdot \binom{N/S}{D}$$

Where:

| Variable | Definition |
|----------|------------|
| $N$ | Total panels across all servers |
| $S$ | Number of comm servers |
| $D$ | Max combo depth (`max_combo_depth`, default 3) |
| $I$ | Refinement passes until no improvement found |
| $\binom{N/S}{D}$ | Combinations function $= \frac{(N/S)!}{D! \cdot (N/S - D)!}$ |

---

## Simplified as N → ∞

$$T(N, S, D) \approx I \cdot \frac{N^D}{S^{D-2} \cdot D!}$$

Big O: $O(N^D)$ — each increment of $D$ raises the entire exponent.

---

## Feasibility Condition

$$\frac{N^D}{I \cdot S^{D-2} \cdot D!} < \tau$$

Where $\tau$ is the operation threshold (default $10 \times 10^9$).

Solve for $N$ to get the breaking point at any given $S$ and $D$.

---

## Breaking Points  $(D=3,\ I=10,\ \tau=10\text{B})$

| Servers $S$ | Max panels $N$ |
|-------------|----------------|
| 2 | ~800 |
| 4 | ~2,400 |
| 6 | ~1,800 |

---

## This Run

| Group | $N$ | $S$ | Est. ops |
|-------|-----|-----|----------|
| Central | 142 | 2 | ~3M |
| Beijing | 22 | 4 | ~60 |
| Chennai | 51 | 6 | ~700 |

The main thing Python will feel different on coming from C#/PowerShell is that **indentation is syntax** — there are no curly braces, the indent level defines code blocks. Get that wrong and it either crashes or does the wrong thing silently. Everything else maps pretty cleanly to what you already know.
